"""Mixing, part C: mixing by instrument (vocal chain to keys and pads). Steps are added to the chapter in ch08_mix.py."""
from hypnomix.app.lessons._common import ALL

STEPS = [
 dict(id="mix-vocal-chain", title="A vocal chain, step by step", daws=ALL,
      say=["The vocal is the part everyone listens to first, so it gets the most care. A **chain** is the list of effects on "
           "the track, in order. There is no single right chain, but there is a sensible order, and each step has a reason.",
           "Start before the effects. Fix problems at the source: edit out clicks and loud breaths, even out the loud and quiet "
           "words with **clip gain** (turning single words or phrases up or down by hand), and soften the worst S sounds by hand "
           "or with a copy of the clip. A compressor can't fix what you can fix with a fader.",
           "Then build the chain one step at a time. Add one effect, set it, switch it on and off at the same loudness, and "
           "keep it only if the voice sounds better, not just louder. Keep it natural: if you hear the processing, you have "
           "gone too far."],
      terms=[["Clip gain", "turning one clip, word or phrase up or down before it reaches any effect."],
             ["Gain riding", "moving the fader up and down by hand, or with automation, so quiet words come forward and loud ones sit back."],
             ["Plate reverb", "a reverb that imitates a large metal sheet: bright, dense and smooth, popular on vocals."],
             ["Slap delay", "a single short echo, about 60-120 ms, that thickens a sound without a clear repeat."],
             ["Vocal chain", "the effects on a vocal track, in the order the sound passes through them."]],
      settings=[["1. Source fixes", "edits, clip gain, hand de-essing", "problems are cheapest to fix before any effect makes them louder"],
                ["2. Low cut", "80-120 Hz, 12 dB per octave", "removes stand rumble and plosive thumps"],
                ["3. Cut mud or boxiness", "a narrow cut of 2-4 dB between 200 and 500 Hz, only if it sounds boxy", "cutting first keeps the compressor from reacting to the mud"],
                ["4. Compression", "2:1 to 4:1, attack 10-30 ms, release 50-150 ms, 3-6 dB of gain reduction", "evens out the line; slower attack keeps the words lively"],
                ["5. De-ess", "5-8 kHz, 3-6 dB on the S's only", "compression can bring the S's forward, so de-ess after it"],
                ["6. Presence and air", "+1 to +2 dB wide at 3-5 kHz; a shelf above 10 kHz, +1 to +2 dB", "clarity last, once the tone is controlled"],
                ["7. Sends", "plate reverb 1.2-2 s, pre-delay 20-40 ms; slap or 1/8-note delay, 20-35% feedback; each with its own EQ (low cut about 200 Hz, high cut about 8 kHz)", "the vocal sits in a space without getting muddy"],
                ["Optional: a second compressor", "parallel, or a gentle one for glue", "adds density only if the first pass left the line uneven"]],
      how={"live": ["Put the effects on the vocal track in this order: **EQ Eight** (low cut and mud cut), **Compressor**, a de-esser set up with a second **Compressor** (see `step:deess`), then a second **EQ Eight** for presence and air.",
                    "Make the reverb and the delay on return tracks (Reverb on A (Hybrid Reverb too, if your edition has it), Delay on B (Echo too, if your edition has it), both 100% wet) and add an **EQ Eight** after each (or **EQ Three** if your edition has no EQ Eight). Send with the vocal's send knobs.",
                    "Clip gain is the **Gain** knob in a clip's Clip view; drawn level moves are Volume automation (press **A** in the Arrangement)."],
           "reaper": ["Click the track's **FX** button and add, in this order: **ReaEQ** (low cut and mud cut), **ReaComp**, **ReaXcomp** (as the de-esser, see `step:deess`), then a second **ReaEQ** for presence and air.",
                      "Make a track for the reverb (**ReaVerbate**) and one for the delay (**ReaDelay**), each 100% wet with a **ReaEQ** after it, and send the vocal to both (route button → Add new send).",
                      "Clip gain is the item volume (right-click the item → Item properties, or its volume handle); drawn moves are Volume envelopes (the track's envelope button → Volume)."],
           "ardour": ["Add an **ACE EQ**, an **ACE Compressor** and a second **ACE EQ** to the vocal strip's plug-in box. For the de-esser, use whatever de-esser or multiband compressor your HypnoMix Studio has, or de-ess by hand on the clip.",
                      "Make buses for the reverb (**ACE Reverb**) and the delay (**ACE Delay**), and add aux sends from the vocal to each.",
                      "Level moves are fader automation (set the track's automation mode to Write or Touch and move the fader)."]},
      listen="After each step, switch the new effect off and on at the same loudness. Ask one question: does the voice sound clearer and "
             "more even, or only processed? At the end, check the vocal against the whole band, not soloed.",
      mistakes=["Adding every effect at once, then not knowing which one helped. Add one at a time and keep only what you can hear helping.",
                "Compressing hard to fix uneven words. Even out the loud and quiet words with clip gain first; the compressor then has less to do. Use fader rides after it for what is still uneven.",
                "Putting the reverb on the vocal track itself, so the room can't be shaped. Use sends, and give the return its own EQ.",
                "Boosting presence and air before taming the mud, so the boost only makes the voice harsh. Cut first, boost last."],
      **{"try": dict(do="Take one lead vocal. Build the chain in the order above, one effect at a time, and each time bypass it and compare at the same loudness. Delete any step you can't hear helping.",
                     success="Your finished chain has fewer steps than the list, every step is one you could defend by ear, and the vocal sounds natural.")},
      quiz=[dict(q="Why put the de-esser after the compressor?", options=["Compression can bring the S's forward, so the de-esser catches them afterwards", "A de-esser always has to be the last effect of all", "It makes the compressor quieter"], answer=0,
                 why="Turning the loud parts down and the whole track up (makeup gain) lifts the S's. De-essing after that deals with them at the level you will actually hear."),
            dict(q="Where does a problem such as a loud breath best get fixed?", options=["With a stronger compressor", "At the source: by editing or clip gain, before any effect", "With extra reverb"], answer=1,
                 why="A fader or an edit fixes it exactly where it is. A compressor treats everything near it and can make the problem louder."),
            dict(q="You boost presence and the voice gets harsh. What is the likely cause?", options=["The reverb is too long", "Mud or harshness was left in below it; cut first, then boost a little", "The low cut is too low"], answer=1,
                 why="A boost raises everything in that range, including a build-up you had not cut. Cut first, boost last, in small steps."),
            ],
      hear=["deess"],
      more=["step:mix-lowcuts", "step:comp", "step:deess", "step:reverb"]),

 dict(id="mix-lead-harmonies", title="Lead vocal and harmonies: stacks, levels and width", daws=ALL,
      say=["A **stack** is a lead vocal with doubles and harmonies behind it. The lead carries the words and the melody; the "
           "others support it. A good stack sounds like one big voice with the lead on top, not like several singers competing.",
           "Keep the lead in the centre. Put the harmonies and doubles behind it in level, and out to the sides in pairs. "
           "A common start is harmonies **3-6 dB under the lead**, and upper harmonies about **1.5 dB under the melody part below them**. "
           "No part should be louder than the part below it unless you chose that on purpose, because the ear follows the highest, "
           "loudest line and will start to hear it as the tune.",
           "Keep it human. Gentle timing and tuning work on the stack is fine, but leave a little looseness: tuned and aligned "
           "to perfection, a stack turns thin and robotic. HypnoMix proposes, you decide, and every tool writes a new file."],
      terms=[["Double", "the lead line sung a second time, panned out to the side to thicken it."],
             ["Harmony", "a part sung at another pitch, usually a third, a fifth or an octave away, to support the lead."],
             ["Stack", "all the vocal parts of a song together: the lead, doubles and harmonies."],
             ["Mirrored pair", "two parts panned the same distance left and right, so the stack stays balanced."]],
      settings=[["Lead vocal", "pan centre", "the anchor of the song"],
                ["Harmonies", "3-6 dB under the lead", "support, not competition"],
                ["Upper harmonies", "about 1.5 dB under the melody part below them", "keeps the ear on the tune"],
                ["Doubles and harmonies", "pan in mirrored pairs, 30-70% each side", "balance and width, with the lead still clear"],
                ["Wide or narrow", "narrow in a verse, wider in a chorus", "the chorus opens up; check it in mono too"],
                ["Tuning and timing", "gentle: only the notes that are clearly off, only partway", "keeps the human feel"]],
      how={"live": ["Give each part its own audio track and put them in a **group** (select the tracks, Cmd+G / Ctrl+G) so the stack gets one fader.",
                    "Set each track's volume relative to the lead by ear, and the pan knobs in mirrored pairs (say L 40% and R 40%).",
                    "To check width, put **Utility** on the group and switch **Mono** on and off."],
           "reaper": ["Give each part its own track, drag them into a folder track (see `step:groups`) and set each volume against the lead.",
                      "Pan in mirrored pairs on the track pan knobs, and check mono by clicking the **Mono** button on the master strip in the Mixer (or by using a mono-sum plug-in) on and off."],
           "ardour": ["Give each part its own track, set the faders against the lead, and pan in mirrored pairs on each strip's panner.",
                      "To check mono, use the mono button in the Monitor section if it is switched on, or a mono-sum plug-in on the master."]},
      listen="Play the verse and the chorus at the same loudness. The lead should be clearly on top, the harmonies should be felt rather than "
             "picked out, and the whole stack should still sound like one voice in mono.",
      mistakes=["A harmony louder than the part below it, so it takes over the tune. Bring it down until the lead leads again.",
                "Panning every part hard left and right: the lead sounds small in the middle. A good start is 30-70% on each side; go wider only when the centre still sounds full.",
                "Tuning and aligning every part to perfection, which makes the stack thin and robotic. Fix only what is clearly off and only partway.",
                "Setting the levels by listening to one line. Check the whole song: a part that is right in the verse can be too loud in the last chorus."],
      **{"try": dict(do="Take a lead and two harmonies. Set the lead alone first, then bring each harmony up until you just notice it, then back it off 1 dB. Pan the harmonies in a mirrored pair and switch to mono.",
                     success="The harmonies are sitting 3-6 dB under the lead, the lead is still easy to follow in mono, and no part is louder than the part below it.")},
      quiz=[dict(q="How loud should an upper harmony sit against the melody part below it?", options=["Louder, so it is heard", "About 1.5 dB under it", "As quiet as possible, 15 dB under"], answer=1,
                 why="Slightly under keeps the ear on the melody. Much quieter and the harmony stops doing its job."),
            dict(q="Where do you put the lead vocal?", options=["Hard left", "Wherever the harmonies are", "In the centre"], answer=2,
                 why="The lead is the anchor. Doubles and harmonies go out to the sides in mirrored pairs."),
            dict(q="Your harmonies are perfectly aligned and tuned and the stack sounds thin. What is a good first move?", options=["Ease the correction back: less strength, only the notes that are clearly off", "Add more reverb", "Pan them to the centre"], answer=0,
                 why="Small differences in timing and pitch are what make several voices sound like several voices. Keep a little of that."),
            ],
      hear=["mono-wide"],
      more=["step:balance", "step:groups", "hypnomix vocallevel", "hypnomix vocalign"]),

 dict(id="mix-drums", title="Drums: kick, snare and the rest", daws=ALL,
      say=["A drum kit is several instruments, each with a job. The **kick** is the weight, the **snare** is the backbeat, the "
           "**toms** are fills, and the **hi-hat** is the pulse. The mix starts with the question 'what is each one for?'",
           "Level and pan follow that. Kick and snare sit in the centre and carry the beat. The hi-hat and toms are panned a little the way "
           "a listener in front of the kit hears them, or the other way round if you want the drummer's view. Hi-hats are bright and "
           "can poke out of the mix, so keep them behind the snare.",
           "If the drums are **samples** (a drum machine, a drum plug-in), they come clean: EQ only to place them. If they are "
           "**recordings** of a real kit, each microphone also picks up the rest of the kit (**bleed**), and microphones on the same "
           "drum can cancel each other. Check the phase before you EQ."],
      terms=[["Kick", "the bass drum: the low thump and the click of the beater."],
             ["Snare", "the drum on the backbeat, with a body and a crack."],
             ["Tom", "a drum between the kick and the cymbals, used for fills."],
             ["Hi-hat", "a pair of small cymbals played with a stick or a foot pedal, usually the steady pulse."],
             ["Close mic", "a microphone placed near one drum, to record mostly that drum."],
             ["Bleed", "the sound of other drums in a close mic."]],
      settings=[["Kick: low cut", "30 Hz (very low)", "removes only what speakers can't play"],
                ["Kick: mud cut", "-2 to -4 dB at 250-400 Hz", "clears the boxiness"],
                ["Kick: click", "+1 to +3 dB at 2-5 kHz", "the beater, so it speaks on small speakers"],
                ["Snare: body", "150-250 Hz, +1 to +2 dB if thin", "weight"],
                ["Snare: crack", "2-5 kHz, +1 to +3 dB", "the attack that cuts through guitars"],
                ["Snare: compression", "3:1-4:1, attack 10-30 ms, 3-6 dB", "a slower attack lets the crack through; the compressor lengthens the sustain"],
                ["Toms", "low cut 60-100 Hz; cut 200-400 Hz if boxy; gate if there is bleed", "weight without the ring of the others"],
                ["Hi-hat", "low cut 300-500 Hz; roll off above 12 kHz if it hisses; pan 20-40%", "keeps it light, behind the snare"]],
      how={"live": ["Put **EQ Eight** on each drum track (or each pad of a **Drum Rack**: open the rack's chain list and add devices to a pad's chain).",
                    "For the kick: band 1 as a low cut near 30 Hz, a bell cut near 300 Hz, a bell boost near 3 kHz. Use a **Compressor** on the snare, attack 10-30 ms.",
                    "Pan the hi-hat and toms with each track's pan knob. For two snare mics, put the **Utility** device on the bottom mic and switch on both of its phase-invert buttons (one for the left channel, one for the right) to flip it, then compare with them off.."],
           "reaper": ["Click the track's **FX** button and add **ReaEQ**, with a low-cut band near 30 Hz, a bell cut near 300 Hz and a bell boost near 3 kHz for the kick.",
                      "Put **ReaComp** on the snare with the attack at 10-30 ms, so the stick's hit gets through before it turns down.",
                      "Pan the tracks on their pan knobs. For two snare mics, press the track's phase invert button (the Ø button in its routing window) on the bottom mic and compare."],
           "ardour": ["Add an **ACE EQ** to each drum strip, and an **ACE Compressor** to the snare.",
                      "Pan on the strip's panner. For two snare mics, press the polarity-invert (Ø) button on the bottom mic's strip and compare."]},
      listen="Play the drums with the rest of the song at the same loudness. The kick should be felt and clear, the snare should land on the "
             "beat without hiding the vocal, and the hi-hat should be there without anyone noticing it.",
      mistakes=["Boosting the kick's low end instead of cutting its mud: it gets bigger in the solo and boomy in the mix. Cut 250-400 Hz first.",
                "Adding a lot of top to the hi-hat until it fizzes. Lower it a decibel and cut the low end instead.",
                "Not checking the phase between the top and bottom snare mics: the snare sounds thin. Flip the polarity of one mic and keep whichever sounds fuller.",
                "Treating samples like recordings: clean samples need little EQ. Place them by level and pan first."],
      **{"try": dict(do="Take a kick and a snare. Cut the kick's mud at 300 Hz by 3 dB, then boost its click at 3 kHz by 2 dB. On the snare, compress with a slow attack, then a fast one, and compare.",
                     success="The kick sounds tighter and clearer in the full mix, and you can hear the snare's crack stay with the slow attack and fade with the fast one.")},
      quiz=[dict(q="Where is the mud on a kick drum, usually?", options=["About 250-400 Hz", "About 8-12 kHz", "Below 30 Hz"], answer=0,
                 why="A cut of a few decibels there takes out the boxy sound without losing the thump or the click."),
            dict(q="Why does a slower compressor attack help a snare?", options=["It makes the snare quieter", "It lets the first crack through before the compressor turns it down", "It removes the bleed"], answer=1,
                 why="The attack time is how long the compressor waits before turning down. A slower attack lets the start of the hit pass, so the crack stays."),
            dict(q="The snare sounds thin when the top and bottom mics play together. What do you check?", options=["Their pan positions", "Their polarity: flip one and compare", "The room reverb"], answer=1,
                 why="The two mics face each other, so they can cancel. Flipping the bottom mic's polarity often brings the body back."),
            ],
      hear=["comp-attack"],
      more=["step:mix-lowcuts", "step:mix-cut-first", "step:mix-gates", "step:groups"]),

 dict(id="mix-drum-room", title="Overheads, room mics and the drum group", daws=ALL,
      say=["On a recorded kit, the **overheads** are microphones above the drums. They carry the cymbals and the picture of the "
           "whole kit. The **room mics** are further away, and they carry the size of the space. Together they tell the listener how big "
           "the kit is and where it sits.",
           "Compress the room mics hard and the sound gets fat: the tails of the room come up and the kit sounds bigger. Blend them "
           "in under the close mics until the kit feels wide, then stop.",
           "Send all the drum tracks to a **drum bus** (a group) and treat the kit as one instrument: a gentle glue compressor, an "
           "EQ, perhaps a little saturation. A second, heavily crushed copy blended in underneath (**parallel compression**) adds "
           "weight without squashing the main sound."],
      terms=[["Overheads", "microphones above the kit that pick up the cymbals and the whole kit."],
             ["Room mics", "microphones placed some distance from the kit to pick up the sound of the room."],
             ["Drum bus", "one track that all the drum tracks are sent to, so they share effects and one fader."],
             ["Glue compression", "light compression on a group, 2-4 dB, that makes the parts sound like one."]],
      settings=[["Overheads: low cut", "150-250 Hz", "the kick and snare have their own mics"],
                ["Room mics: compressor", "4:1 or higher, fast attack, 6-10 dB of gain reduction", "brings up the room's tail for a fat sound"],
                ["Room mics: level", "blended in until the kit feels wide, usually well under the overheads", "size, not mess"],
                ["Drum bus: glue compressor", "2:1-4:1, slow attack 10-30 ms, auto or medium release, 2-4 dB", "holds the kit together"],
                ["Drum bus: EQ", "a gentle shape, 1-2 dB", "tone for the whole kit"],
                ["Drum bus: saturation", "a little, blended in", "warmth and weight"],
                ["Parallel crush", "a copy on a send, heavily compressed, blended in underneath", "density with the punch kept"]],
      how={"live": ["Select the drum tracks and group them with **Cmd+G (Ctrl+G)** (see `step:groups`). Put **Glue Compressor** (or **Compressor**) and **EQ Eight** on the group track.",
                    "On the overheads, **EQ Eight** with a low cut at about 200 Hz. On the room mics, a **Compressor** with a fast attack and 6-10 dB of gain reduction.",
                    "For parallel compression, send the drums to a return track with a heavily set **Compressor** (see `step:mix-comp-parallel`)."],
           "reaper": ["Select the drum tracks, then right-click one of them → **Move tracks to folder** → **New folder track** (see `step:groups`). Put **ReaComp** and **ReaEQ** on the folder track.",
                      "Overheads: **ReaEQ**, band 1 as a high-pass at about 200 Hz. Room mics: **ReaComp** with a fast attack and 6-10 dB of gain reduction.",
                      "For parallel compression, send the folder to a track with a heavily set **ReaComp**."],
           "ardour": ["Add a bus for the drums (Track → Add Track, Bus or VCA…), send each drum track to it, and put **ACE Compressor** and **ACE EQ** on the bus.",
                      "Overheads: **ACE High/Low Pass Filter** at about 200 Hz. Room mics: **ACE Compressor** with a fast attack.",
                      "For parallel compression, make a second bus and send the drum bus to it with an aggressive compressor."]},
      listen="Switch the room mics in and out at the same loudness: the kit should get bigger and more present, not messier or more distant. "
             "Check that the overheads and the close mics together don't thin the sound.",
      mistakes=["Too much room: the drums wash out and lose their punch. Bring the room mics down until the close mics are clear again.",
                "Skipping the phase check between the overheads and the close mics: the kit sounds thin. Flip one polarity and compare, or nudge the tracks in time.",
                "A drum bus compressor with a fast attack: the transients vanish. Use 10-30 ms.",
                "Setting the parallel track as loud as the original. Blend it in underneath and listen for weight, not squash."],
      **{"try": dict(do="On a recorded kit, bring up the overheads under the close mics. Flip the overheads' polarity and listen. Then add the room mics, compress them hard, and blend them in.",
                     success="You can say which polarity is fuller, the kit sounds bigger with the room mics in, and the drums still punch.")},
      quiz=[dict(q="What does compressing the room mics do?", options=["Removes the cymbals", "Brings up the room's tail so the kit sounds fatter", "Makes the kick louder"], answer=1,
                 why="Heavy compression turns the loud hits down and lets the quiet room sound come up behind them."),
            dict(q="Why low-cut the overheads at 150-250 Hz?", options=["The kick and snare already have close mics for their low end", "Cymbals have no high frequencies", "It makes them wider"], answer=0,
                 why="Everything below that in the overheads is boom from the kit, which muddies the mix. The close mics already carry it."),
            ],
      hear=["polarity"],
      more=["step:groups", "step:mix-comp-parallel", "step:mix-drums"]),

 dict(id="mix-bass-kick", title="Bass and kick together", daws=ALL,
      say=["The kick and the bass both live in the low end, and the low end has room for only one big thing at a time. If they play "
           "the same notes together, they add, cancel and smear. The fix is to decide that **one of them owns the sub**, the deepest octave, "
           "and give the other a different place.",
           "A common split: the kick gets its thump at about 60-80 Hz, and a click at 3-5 kHz so it speaks on small speakers. The bass keeps its "
           "body at 80-200 Hz. Cut a little of the bass where the kick thumps, or the other way round, or duck the bass for a moment on every kick.",
           "Also check the **phase** at the low end: two low sounds that are out of step can cancel and leave a hole. Keep everything below about 120 Hz "
           "in the centre (mono): the low end carries the power, and it doesn't need width."],
      terms=[["Sub", "the deepest bass, below about 60-80 Hz, felt as much as heard."],
             ["Low end", "the bass and kick region of the mix, roughly 30-200 Hz."],
             ["Carving", "cutting a little from one part where another part needs room."],
             ["Bass mono", "making everything below a chosen frequency mono."]],
      settings=[["Kick thump", "60-80 Hz, +1 to +3 dB", "the weight of each hit"],
                ["Kick click", "3-5 kHz, +1 to +3 dB", "audible on small speakers"],
                ["Bass body", "80-200 Hz", "the note itself, which is clear on most speakers"],
                ["Carving", "-2 to -3 dB on the bass at the kick's thump, or on the kick at the bass's body", "choose who owns the sub, cut the other"],
                ["Sidechain", "bass ducked 2-4 dB on every kick, release timed to the next kick", "kick and bass stop fighting without audible pumping"],
                ["Mono below", "about 100-120 Hz", "tight, powerful low end that survives any speaker"]],
      how={"live": ["Carving: put **EQ Eight** on the bass with a bell cut at the kick's thump frequency, or a second on the kick.",
                    "Sidechain: put a **Compressor** on the bass, open its **Sidechain** section, switch it on, and choose the kick track in the **Audio From** menu. Lower the threshold until the bass ducks on each kick.",
                    "Mono low end: put **Utility** on the bass (or the master), switch **Bass Mono** on and set the frequency to about 120 Hz.",
                    "Phase: on the bass track's **Utility** (not the master's), switch on both phase-invert buttons, left and right, to flip the whole signal, and compare with them off. Flip one of the two tracks, not both."],
           "reaper": ["Carving: add a **ReaEQ** band as a bell cut on the bass at the kick's thump (or a second one on the kick at the bass's).",
                      "Sidechain: set the bass track to 4 channels (its route button), send the kick to it on channels 3/4 (the send's channel setting), then in **ReaComp** set the detector input to auxiliary inputs and lower the threshold until the bass ducks on each kick.",
                      "Mono low end: on the master, HypnoMix Main's **Build chain** includes bass mono below 120 Hz.",
                      "Phase: use the bass track's phase invert button (the Ø button in its routing window) to flip and compare. Flip one of the two tracks, not both."],
           "ardour": ["Carving: add an **ACE EQ** to the bass and cut where the kick thumps.",
                      "Sidechain: use **ACE Compressor**'s sidechain input, fed by a send from the kick strip (see its manual for the routing).",
                      "Phase: use the strip's polarity-invert (Ø) button."]},
      listen="Play kick and bass together at the same loudness, then in mono. Every kick should land with a clear thump and the bass line "
             "should still be easy to follow underneath.",
      mistakes=["Boosting both at the same frequency: the low end is loud and indistinct. Pick one to own it and cut a little from the other.",
                "Sidechaining so hard that the bass pumps audibly. Aim for 2-4 dB and a release that is back before the next kick.",
                "Judging the low end on small speakers or earbuds only: they can't reproduce it. Use a set of speakers or headphones that go low, and check on small ones afterwards.",
                "Leaving wide stereo in the bass: the low end gets weak in mono and on a club system. Make it mono below 100-120 Hz."],
      **{"try": dict(do="Play the kick and the bass together. Cut 3 dB from the bass at the kick's thump and listen. Then undo it and try a sidechain from the kick onto the bass instead, 2-4 dB. Run `hypnomix masking` on your stems folder to see which parts fight.",
                     success="The kick and the bass are each clear, and you know which method (carving or sidechain) you preferred and why.")},
      quiz=[dict(q="Two parts play the same low notes together. What is a sensible first move?", options=["Boost both for more weight", "Pick one to own the sub and cut a little from the other", "Pan them to opposite sides"], answer=1,
                 why="The low end can only have one big thing at a time. Cutting where the parts overlap restores clarity."),
            dict(q="Why keep the low end in mono?", options=["It makes it sound brighter", "Left and right can cancel at low frequencies, and it needs to hit the same on every speaker", "It uses less disk space"], answer=1,
                 why="Low notes are long waves that do not localise, so width adds nothing and risks cancellation. Mono keeps the power."),
            dict(q="What does a sidechain from the kick to the bass do?", options=["Turns the bass down briefly on every kick", "Turns the kick up", "Pans the bass away"], answer=0,
                 why="The kick triggers the compressor on the bass, which ducks it for a moment so the kick gets the space."),
            ],
      hear=["kick-bass"],
      more=["step:mix-masking", "step:mix-sidechain", "hypnomix masking", "hypnomix sidechain"]),

 dict(id="mix-guitars", title="Guitars", daws=ALL,
      say=["Guitars fill the middle of the mix and share it with the vocal, the snare and the keys. The job of the mix is to give them "
           "character without letting them cover the singer.",
           "An **acoustic guitar** needs a low cut to clear the boom, a gentle compressor to even out the strumming, and a little reverb. Keep "
           "its body: don't thin it too far. An **electric guitar** is about the mid-range: that is where it speaks, at 1-3 kHz, "
           "and also where the vocal lives. Lower the high fizz, and leave room for the voice.",
           "Guitars are often **double-tracked**: the same part played twice, then panned one left and one right. That makes them wide "
           "and leaves the centre free for the vocal. Clean guitars need a light touch; distorted guitars are already dense, so they need more cutting than boosting."],
      terms=[["Double-tracking", "playing the same part twice on two separate tracks, panned left and right, for a wide sound."],
             ["Rhythm guitar", "the guitar that plays the chords and the groove."],
             ["Lead guitar", "the guitar that plays the solos and the melodic lines."],
             ["Fizz", "harsh high frequencies on a distorted guitar, roughly above 5-6 kHz."]],
      settings=[["Acoustic: low cut", "80-120 Hz", "removes boom; bass and kick own the low notes"],
                ["Acoustic: compressor", "2:1-3:1, attack 10-30 ms, 2-4 dB", "evens out the strum and keeps the body"],
                ["Acoustic: reverb", "a slap delay or a small plate, quietly", "space without distance"],
                ["Electric: low cut", "80-100 Hz", "keeps the amp's weight, loses the mud"],
                ["Electric: mid-range", "focus 1-3 kHz; a small cut of 1-3 dB there to make room for the vocal if they fight", "where it speaks; leaves the voice clear"],
                ["Electric: high roll-off", "a gentle cut above 6-8 kHz", "removes fizz"],
                ["Double-tracked pair", "one left, one right, 50-100%", "wide, with the centre left clear"],
                ["Rhythm and lead", "rhythm at the sides; lead nearer the centre or up in level for solos", "each does its job"]],
      how={"live": ["**EQ Eight** on each guitar track: band 1 as a low cut at 80-120 Hz; a gentle high-shelf or high-cut above 6-8 kHz for electric; a bell near 2 kHz to place the mid-range.",
                    "Acoustic: **Compressor**, ratio 2:1-3:1, attack 10-30 ms. Send it to a return with a short **Reverb** or **Echo**.",
                    "Double-tracked parts: pan one track left and the other right on the pan knobs."],
           "reaper": ["**ReaEQ** on each guitar track: band 1 as a high-pass at 80-120 Hz, a high-shelf or low-pass above 6-8 kHz for electric, a bell near 2 kHz.",
                      "Acoustic: **ReaComp**, ratio 2:1-3:1, attack 10-30 ms, and a send to a short **ReaVerbate**.",
                      "Pan the double-tracked parts left and right on the track pan knobs."],
           "ardour": ["**ACE High/Low Pass Filter** or **ACE EQ** on each guitar track for the low cut and the high roll-off.",
                      "Acoustic: **ACE Compressor**, with a send to a bus with **ACE Reverb**.",
                      "Pan the double-tracked parts left and right on each strip's panner."]},
      listen="Solo the guitar only to hear its character, then listen with the vocal. The vocal should still be easy to follow, and the guitar "
             "should sound full without being the loudest thing.",
      mistakes=["Leaving the full low end on the guitars: the mix gets muddy. Low cut at 80-120 Hz.",
                "Boosting the guitar's mid-range in the solo until it sounds great, then losing the vocal. Check it in the full mix.",
                "Panning a single guitar hard to one side: the mix leans. Pair it with something, or keep it near 50%.",
                "Compressing a distorted guitar hard: it is already compressed by the distortion, and it just loses life. Use little or none."],
      **{"try": dict(do="With the vocal playing, sweep a 3 dB cut across 1-3 kHz on a guitar track until the vocal's words become clearer. Leave the cut at that frequency.",
                     success="You can hear the lyrics more clearly with the cut on, and the guitar still sounds like itself.")},
      quiz=[dict(q="Why are electric guitars often double-tracked and panned left and right?", options=["It makes them quieter", "It makes them wide and leaves the centre free for the vocal", "It removes the need for EQ"], answer=1,
                 why="Two separate performances panned apart sound wide and big, and the centre stays clear for the lead vocal."),
            dict(q="Where does an electric guitar mostly speak?", options=["Around 1-3 kHz", "Below 80 Hz", "Above 12 kHz"], answer=0,
                 why="The mid-range is where its character is, and also where the vocal lives, so that is where they need to share."),
            ],
      more=["step:mix-masking", "step:mix-panning", "step:mix-lowcuts"]),

 dict(id="mix-keys-pads", title="Keys, pads and synths", daws=ALL,
      say=["Keys and synths can be anything from a few notes to a wall of sound. A **piano** needs a low cut to leave the bass alone and some "
           "presence in the middle so that it doesn't vanish. A **pad** (a long, slow, smooth sound) is a different problem: it is full of "
           "low and mid energy, it holds notes for a long time, and so it can mask almost everything else.",
           "Take pads and long synth notes out of the way of the lead: cut the low end hard (about **120 Hz**) and carve out the vocal "
           "region with a gentle dip around **1-3 kHz**. A wide stereo pad also leaves the centre free. If the pad still gets in the way, "
           "duck it from the vocal with a sidechain.",
           "Put reverb on keys for space, but go easy on the pad, since many pads already have a reverb built in. Check it dry first. Keep long notes from hiding the lead and let the "
           "level move: a little more in the chorus, a little less in the verse."],
      terms=[["Pad", "a long, smooth, sustained synth or string sound that fills the background."],
             ["Sustain", "the part of a note that holds after the start."],
             ["Stereo width", "how far left and right a sound spreads."]],
      settings=[["Piano: low cut", "60-100 Hz", "leaves the low end to the bass"],
                ["Piano: presence", "+1 to +2 dB at 2-4 kHz", "it reads in the mix"],
                ["Pads: low cut", "about 120 Hz, 12-24 dB per octave", "removes the mud that masks everything"],
                ["Pads: vocal carve", "-2 to -4 dB, wide, at 1-3 kHz", "leaves the voice clear"],
                ["Pads: width", "wide stereo", "leaves the centre for the lead"],
                ["Sidechain to the vocal", "2-3 dB of ducking", "the pad gets out of the way only when the singer sings"],
                ["Reverb on keys", "a short plate or room, quietly", "space; none on a pad that has its own"],
                ["Level automation", "up 1-2 dB in the chorus", "the arrangement opens up"]],
      how={"live": ["**EQ Eight** on each track: band 1 as a low cut (about 120 Hz for pads, lower for piano) and a wide bell cut at 1-3 kHz on the pad.",
                    "Width: put **Utility** on the pad and raise **Width** above 100%.",
                    "Sidechain to the vocal: put a **Compressor** on the pad, open its **Sidechain** section, switch it on and choose the vocal track in **Audio From**.",
                    "Chorus lift: press **A** in the Arrangement and draw the track's Volume up by 1-2 dB in the chorus."],
           "reaper": ["**ReaEQ** on each track: band 1 as a high-pass (about 120 Hz for pads, lower for piano) and a wide bell cut at 1-3 kHz on the pad.",
                      "Width: REAPER's JS effects include stereo-width tools (type stereo in the FX browser's search box); widen the pad a little, and check it in mono.",
                      "Sidechain to the vocal: set the pad track to 4 channels, send the vocal to it on channels 3/4 and set **ReaComp**'s detector input to auxiliary inputs.",
                      "Chorus lift: show the track's Volume envelope (its envelope button → Volume) and draw it up by 1-2 dB in the chorus."],
           "ardour": ["**ACE High/Low Pass Filter** or **ACE EQ** for the low cut and the carve.",
                      "Sidechain to the vocal: use **ACE Compressor**'s sidechain input, fed from the vocal strip.",
                      "Chorus lift: use fader automation (Write or Touch) to raise the level in the chorus."]},
      listen="Play the section with the vocal at the same loudness with the pad on and off. The vocal should be clearer with the pad "
             "carved, and the pad should still feel big around it.",
      mistakes=["Leaving a full-range pad in the mix: the low end gets muddy and the vocal loses clarity. Low cut it at about 120 Hz.",
                "Carving too deep: the pad disappears and sounds hollow. A 2-4 dB dip is usually enough.",
                "Adding reverb to a pad that already has a big built-in space. Check it dry first.",
                "Setting the keys once and leaving them: the chorus sounds the same as the verse. Automate a small lift."],
      **{"try": dict(do="Put a low cut at 120 Hz on a pad and a 3 dB wide dip at 2 kHz. Play it under the lead vocal, bypass the EQ, and compare at the same loudness.",
                     success="With the EQ on, the vocal's words are clearer and the low end is tighter, and the pad still sounds full.")},
      quiz=[dict(q="Why do pads need a hard low cut?", options=["They have no bass", "They hold long, full-range notes that mask the bass, the guitars and the vocal", "It makes them louder"], answer=1,
                 why="Long notes with low and mid energy sit under everything else and blur it. Cutting the lows at about 120 Hz gives that space back."),
            dict(q="What does a sidechain from the vocal to a pad do?", options=["Raises the pad when the vocal is silent", "Turns the pad down slightly while the vocal is singing", "Pans the pad away"], answer=1,
                 why="The vocal triggers the pad's compressor, so the pad ducks a little while the singer sings and comes back in the gaps."),
            dict(q="Where do you carve a pad to leave room for the vocal?", options=["Around 1-3 kHz", "Below 40 Hz", "Above 15 kHz"], answer=0,
                 why="That range holds the voice's clarity, so a gentle dip in the pad leaves the words clear."),
            ],
      more=["step:mix-masking", "step:mix-sidechain", "step:automation"]),
]

